2.The code uses the following data structures:
TreeMap: This is used in the SubImgCharMatcher class to store the character brightness ,
The TreeMap ensures that the characters are sorted by their brightness values.
(get-O(1),bild-O(nlogn),aad/remove-O(logn),space-O(n)) we chse this becus we will yose mostly get by brightns.
List: This is used in the ImageProcessor class to store a list of sub-images that
are created by splitting the original image.(acses by index-O(1),blid O(n)avrege),we chose bcuse its simple
and efficient way to store a collection of sub-images.
Set: This is used in the AsciiArtAlgorithm class to store the character set that the SubImgCharMatcher will
use to match brightness values to characters.
We chose this data structure because it maintains the members in the order in which they are addressed by
index with complexity O(1) and is convenient for presenting the Husky characters available to the user with
low complexity input O(NLOGN) and cheap output and with minimal space complexity.


3. We classified the problems in the code into three types of anomalies:
A. A problem with the command format.
B. A problem in execute of the operation.
C. A request for a resolution that cannot be displayed.
For each problem we wrote an appropriate Exception class which itself inherits
from the Exception class, we threw the errors during the code according to the
classification, caught them in the RUN method and printed an error accordingly,
for invalid image input we continued to chain the problem to the main function
and from there we made sure to close the program.

4.We added a sterer to the SubImgCharMatcher class for a field with which we decided
how to round the value in a function that finds the husky character whose brightness
is closest to a certain value.